@page "/docs/accessibility/audit-fixes"

@* With a free account: the component, the fix, the file and line of a finding (AuditTrace.cs); --recheck (AuditVerdicts.cs);
   the agent's tools audit_page and recheck_finding (BuildTools.cs) and the skill's "Check accessibility" (Agent/SKILL.md).
   The audit changes no code: --fix is paused (Nov 25, 2026). *@

<ArticlePage Section="accessibility" Slug="audit-fixes">
    <Lead>
        With a free account, <Code>izzy audit</Code> says which component drew a finding's element, what to
        change and where it is written in your code. You or your coding agent make the change, and a recheck says
        whether it worked. izzy itself changes no code.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">Find a finding in your code</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Each finding names the IzzyBlazor component that drew the element, links its docs
            page, and gives the fix when izzy knows one, such as the option to set. With <Code>--run</Code> or
            <Code>--project</Code>, izzy also reads the app's source and gives the file and line where the
            component is written:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="text" Title="report.md">@TraceLines</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            izzy finds the page's file from its <Code>@@page</Code> route, then looks in that file, its layout and
            the components of yours they use. One tag is that line. Among several, it picks the one whose written
            text is in the element, such as its <Code>Label</Code> or <Code>id</Code>, and says how sure it is:
            high for one tag, medium for one picked from several. When it can't pick, it lists the places.
            An element a component draws for you, such as a button in a DataGrid's toolbar, is traced to that
            component's tag. Plain HTML is found by its tag with its <Code>id</Code> or <Code>class</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            Some components draw the same element, such as Dialog and Drawer, or Stack and Row. Without the
            source the report names each of them, and the source says which one it is. What a component draws
            inside itself, such as a date field's calendar button or a DataGrid's pager, is traced to that
            component. What you wrote inside a component, such as a Button as a Menu's
            <Code>Trigger</Code>, is traced to your own tag.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">What the fix is, and isn't</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            A fix is a sentence about the rule that failed: the option to set, or the markup to change. It makes
            that finding go away for the right reason. It doesn't tell you the words to use. A name such as
            <Code>Label="Order path"</Code> has to say what the thing is to someone who can't see it, and only a
            person who knows the page can write it.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Check a fix</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Each finding <Code>report.md</Code> shows has an id that stays the same from run to run, and
            <Code>results.json</Code> has every finding's. After a fix,
            <Code>--recheck</Code> audits the finding's page again and says whether it is fixed. izzy finds the
            page in the newest run in <Code>.izzy/audit/</Code> that has the finding:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="bash">izzy audit --recheck 527e645e33f7 --run</Code>
        <Table Items="Answers" Caption="What a recheck answers" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params site-params-prose" Bordered="false">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Answer" Width="Length.Rem(10)">@context!.Name</TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Exit code" Width="Length.Rem(6)">@context!.Code</TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Means">@context!.Text</TemplateColumn>
            </Columns>
        </Table>
        <TextBlock>
            An id is made from the rule, the page and the element's selector, so an edit that moves the element
            gives its finding a new id. izzy then says how many findings of the same rule the earlier run lacks,
            and names one.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            Fixed means the rule no longer fails on that element. For a name, a label or an announcement, listen
            to it with a screen reader as well.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">With a coding agent</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            <Anchor Href="docs/tools/ai-assistants">izzy mcp</Anchor> gives a coding agent two tools for
            this. <Code>audit_page</Code> audits one page and answers with the findings by verdict and where the
            report is. <Code>recheck_finding</Code> takes a finding's id and says whether it is fixed. Neither
            changes code: the agent or you make the fix. The tools need a free account, and audit one page a call.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            The skill that <Code>izzy agent</Code> writes tells the agent how to use them: fix the real findings,
            ask you about each one that needs a person, never accept a finding by itself, and never say a page
            conforms because an audit found nothing.
        </TextBlock>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    private sealed record Row(string Name, string Code, string Text);

    private static readonly Row[] Answers =
    [
        new("Fixed", "0", "The page was audited again and the finding is gone, or it is there with a verdict of false positive or accepted."),
        new("Still there", "5", "The finding is on the page again."),
        new("Not checked", "1", "The page didn't load, answered with an error or led to another page, or you asked for other widths than the earlier run's."),
    ];

    private const string TraceLines = """
        - Component: `Breadcrumb`, inside `PageHeader`
        - Fix: Two Breadcrumbs on a page need a name each: set Label on this one, such as Label="Order path". [Docs](https://izzyblazor.com/docs/components/breadcrumb)
        - Source: `Components/Pages/Orders.razor:12`, confidence high
        """;
}
